## Purpose
This question evaluates practical planning and tool orchestration skills using LangChain for real-world data processing workflows.

## Key Concepts
- LangChain Tools, agents, and planning patterns
- Tool execution order: fetch, parse, compute
- Error handling strategies: retries with backoff, timeouts, and graceful degradation

## Code Example
```javascript
import { ChatOpenAI } from 'langchain/chat_models';
import { AgentExecutor, createOpenAIFunctionsAgent } from 'langchain/agents';
import { S3Tool } from './tools/s3';
import { DataProcessorTool } from './tools/processor';

const s3Tool = new S3Tool();
const processorTool = new DataProcessorTool();

const tools = [s3Tool, processorTool];
const model = new ChatOpenAI({ temperature: 0 });
const prompt = ...;

const agent = await createOpenAIFunctionsAgent({ model, tools, prompt });
const executor = new AgentExecutor({ agent, tools, maxIterations: 3 });

const result = await executor.invoke({ 
  input: 'Generate a daily KPI report from s3://bucket/data/daily.csv'
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this architecture to handle multiple files in batch processing?
- How would you implement testing for the planner with unreliable S3 access?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to architect a scalable cross-lingual RAG pipeline handling chunking, multilingual embeddings, and vector stores with versioning and TTL. It also probes cross-language retrieval strategies and evaluation plans to reduce hallucinations.

## Key Concepts
- Multilingual embeddings (LaBSE, mBERT) and language tagging
- Document-type aware chunking strategies
- Vector DB indexing with versioning, TTL/decay, and provenance
- Cross-language retrieval + fallback translation
- Evaluation: latency, recall across languages, hallucination rate

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: bilingual embedder + cross-language retrieve
const embedder = new LaBSEEmbedder({ model: 'labse' })
const index = new VectorDB({ name: 'docs', ... })
async function ingest(doc){
  const chunks = chunkDoc(doc, typeRules[doc.type])
  for (const c of chunks){
    index.upsert({ id: c.id, vec: embedder.embed(c.text), meta: { lang: c.lang, version: doc.version, ts: doc.ts }})
  }
}
async function query(q, lang){
  const v = embedder.embed(q)
  const hits = index.query(v, { k: 5, lang })
  // cross-language re-rank can involve translating top hits or language-agnostic scoring
  return hits
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle new languages or drift in embeddings?
- How would you measure cross-language retrieval effectiveness in production?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to implement a minimal retrieval loop that works with embeddings, chunking, and a vector-like store, while considering multilingual routing and update freshness.

## Key Concepts

- In-memory vector store
- Text chunking with overlap
- Embedding generation
- Cosine similarity search
- Multilingual handling
- Update freshness and caching

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo JS snippet: minimal in-memory index
const model = loadModel('all-MiniLM-L6-v2');
function chunk(text, max=300, overlap=50){ /* split and overlap */ }
let index = [];
function indexDocs(docs){
  docs.forEach(doc=>{
    chunk(doc.text).forEach(ch=>{
      index.push({id: doc.id+':' + hash(ch), text: ch, emb: model.encode(ch)});
    });
  });
}
function query(q, topK=5){
  const qEmb = model.encode(q);
  return index
    .map(n => ({...n, score: cosine(n.emb, qEmb)}))
    .sort((a,b)=> b.score-a.score)
    .slice(0, topK);
}
```

