## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of attention scaling, memory constraints, and practical trade-offs in production-grade transformers.

## Key Concepts
- Block-local attention and memory scaling
- Attention masking within a window
- Q/K/V reshaping for block processing
- Trade-offs: accuracy vs latency vs memory

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: local attention in blocks
function localAttention(Q, K, V, block=256, window=128){
  // Q,K,V: [B, T, D]
  const B = Q.length, T = Q[0].length, D = Q[0][0].length;
  let O = Array.from({length:B}, () => Array.from({length:T}, () => new Array(D).fill(0)));
  for (let b=0; b<B; b++){
    for (let s=0; s<T; s+=block){
      const end = Math.min(T, s+block);
      for (let t=s; t<end; t++){
        const wStart = Math.max(0, t-window);
        const wEnd = Math.min(T, t+window+1);
        // compute attention over [wStart, wEnd)
        // A = softmax((Q[t]·K[w].T)/sqrt(D))
        // O[b][t] = sum_{w=wStart}^{wEnd-1} A[w]·V[w]
      }
    }
  }
  return O;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does changing window size affect long-range dependency capture and memory?
- What metrics would you monitor to detect degradation when switching from global to local attention?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests focus on practical evaluation rather than high-level theory. This question probes ability to design simple actionable metrics, masking rules, and small-scale experiments, all in a beginner-friendly way that translates to real-world translation pipelines.

## Key Concepts

- Boundary-aware attention
- Subword tokenization
- Word-level alignment
- Simple masking strategies
- Small datasets and quick experiments

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal example to compute anchor-based metric
function computeAnchorMetric(attn, starts) {
  // attn: 2D array [t_len][s_len]
  // starts: Set of source indices that are word starts
  let score = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t < attn.length; t++) {
    let sumAnchor = 0;
    for (let s = 0; s < attn[t].length; s++) {
      if (starts.has(s)) sumAnchor += attn[t][s];
    }
    score += sumAnchor;
  }
  return attn.length ? score / attn.length : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to multilingual or morphologically rich languages?
- How would you validate with human annotations?
